  "notes": "Rap-vocal dance-pop crossover — the most prolific feature vocalist of the 2010s EDM-pop boom. ⛔ This is one of the volume's named multi-mode entries, written once here with the `crunk`-lane origin folded in as a mode, and the compendium's own generator note is why the two are modes rather than a blend: \"Mode A is a dembow/clave model at 92-104; the dance-pop mode above is four-on-the-floor at 126-130. Different rhythmic families — pick one, never blend.\" The era spans both windows the entry gives (2009-2016 for the dance-pop lane, 2004-2010 for the Miami origin). The base blocks are the crossover: \"electro-house/Dutch-house grammar — four-square kick, hard clap on 2&4, 16th hats, riser-and-snare builds\", with the arrangement model the entry states — \"rapped verses over the producer's loop with a sung guest chorus and an instrumental drop… he is the rhythmic element, not the melodic one\" — so the melody is a low, dense, front-of-beat rap line and the hook belongs to the guest. The `miami dembow` mode carries the documented cell: kick on 1 and the \"&\" of 2, snare/clap on 2 & 4 with the offbeat \"and-a\" figure, 16th hats, timbale/conga fills, and an 808 sub plus a mid-register synth bass playing the dembow rhythm — which is why that mode is the only place `bass808` appears, authored as `counter_riff` so the mid-register bass part survives. Its harmony (i-VI-VII and i-VII-VI four-bar vamps) and its higher note density are the entry's own. ⚠ The research marks the crossover BPM band `[LOW-CONF]` on Mode A and calls the drum and bass parameters electro-house/Dutch-house convention; both marks are repeated here. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all velocities, ghost placements, perc densities and slide probabilities. ⚠ **The countermelody reached 919/1000 distinct answers against a floor of 980 (2026-08-17, counter variety floor).** The 1-bar value came out of `melody.phraseBars`, which is now [2,4] weighted [4,3]. `generators::melody` writes a phrase's rhythm **once** and reuses it for every repeat, so a 1-bar phrase gives the whole clip one bar of rhythmic entropy — and the counter, whose onsets are read off the lead's, inherits that ceiling. Distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count. ⚠ The value that moved here is INFERRED rather than a researched claim — the entry's Melody/chords line reads \"the arrangement model is rapped verses over the producer's loop with a sung guest chorus and an instrumental drop — he is the rhythmic element, not the melodic one. Toplines are delivered as fast bilingual couplets front-of-beat; the hook is the guest's.\", and every claim in it still holds after the change. ⚠ **The countermelody still reached only 979/1000 against the floor of 980 (2026-08-17).** Forced to one style at a time over 1,000 seeds, `octave_echo` measures 1000 and `answer_lick` 967: `generators::counter` writes an answer lick only inside the last beat of each bar, and this lead is a dense front-of-beat rap line at 8–14 notes a bar, so that beat is usually already attacked and the lick has nowhere to go. The weights are swapped to `octave_echo` 3 against `answer_lick` 2, which is also the more faithful reading — the entry says \"he is the rhythmic element, not the melodic one … the hook is the guest’s\", so the answering layer on these records is the producer’s \"bright saw-lead chorus\" restating that hook rather than a sung phrase-answer of his own. Measured 979 → 991 distinct per 1,000. ⚠ The weights are INFERRED; the saw-lead hook and the rhythmic-not-melodic role are the entry’s own.",
